  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nRain showers move into Northeastern Minnesota by this evening\nbringing dry conditions back to the region. A weak cold front\ntonight will bring some clearing to areas west of the Red River\nValley allowing temperatures to cool into the 40s F with low to\nmid 50s F everywhere else Sunday morning. Surface high pressure\nmoves in Sunday pushing out the clouds and bringing \nhigh temperatures in the mid 60s to low 70s F. High pressure \n(1020 mb) Sunday night will bring cooler temperatures in the 40s\nF across the region Monday morning.\n\nMonday, a 500 mb wave begins to track across the Northern \nPlains bringing our next chance for precipitation. Southerly \nwinds will advect in anomalously high moisture with PWAT values \nbeing >200% of normal. Southerly winds advecting in warmer air, \nhigh pressure to the north setting up a baroclinic zone, and \npotentially a deformation zone will provide the forcing for this\nprecipitation. The weak low pressure system is expected to \ntrack generally along a line from Jamestown to Roseau, aligning \nwell with the areas that can expect the heaviest rainfall. \nEastern North Dakota and Northwest Minnesota currently have a \n50-80% chance for getting greater than 1 inch of rain. \nProbabilities fall into the 10-30% range for 2 inches of rain, \nwith those values being closer to the heaviest axis.\nPrecipitation chances are expected to gradually decrease\nthroughout the day Tuesday.\n\nThis rain is expected to bring drought relief but could cause \nissues as well. Slow moving showers with embedded thunderstorms\nin such a moist environment have the potential to cause \nisolated flooding. Due to this, the Weather Prediction Center \nhas issued a level 1 of 4 risk for excessive rainfall. Severe \nstorms are not expected. Heavy rain during harvest time will \nalso impact agricultural activities. \n\nThe weather pattern for the middle to late week period is less\ncertain but looks to be more benign with seasonable temperatures\nand limited precipitation chances.",
